## 2. Test methodology

### 2.1 What the two simulations measure

| Aspect | `InteropSimulation` | `RecordedSimulation` |
|---|---|---|
| **Target** | `/interop/games/play` only | Full Nginx → webapp → users → game → gamey chain |
| **Auth** | None (interop is public) | JWT, issued on login |
| **Peak concurrency** | 14 virtual users | 30 virtual users |
| **Duration** | 2 m 59 s | 3 m 13 s |
| **Request count** | 2 463 | 4 480 |
| **Objective** | Find the engine's compute bottleneck, quantify 503 rate per bot class | Find the concurrency at which the full-stack user experience degrades |

### 2.2 Why two simulations instead of one

A single Gatling simulation cannot answer both of our questions cleanly. The two workloads we want to measure have fundamentally incompatible measurement requirements:
- **Incompatible Workload Models:** The interop test requires an open model (pushing a fixed rate of requests regardless of engine speed) to reveal the exact rate at which 503s trigger. The recorded test requires a closed model (holding a fixed number of concurrent users) so the server dictates throughput, revealing true user latency. Mixing them would average fast webapp responses (~50ms) with heavy engine computations (~3s), creating a meaningless global p95.
- **Incompatible Failure Semantics:** In the interop test, a 503 is an expected outcome (successful load-shedding). In the recorded webapp test, a 503 is a critical system failure (a user couldn't play their first move). A unified simulation cannot apply both interpretations to the same status code simultaneously.

### 2.3 Architectural lessons baked into the scripts

It is worth noting that several attempts had to be done untill the load test was carried out correctly. This involved some architectural issues needing fixing relative to earlier runs. Some are the following:

- **503 camouflage** — earlier runs used `.check(status().in(200, 503))` without grouping, so the ~5 ms fast-reject 503s and the ~3 000 ms legitimate engine responses were averaged together in the report. Every play request in `InteropSimulation` now saves the HTTP status and is wrapped in `group(session -> "<Label>_" + status)`, producing separate `*_200` and `*_503` buckets.
- **409 cascade** — the recorder originally captured 12 sequential `POST /move` calls with hard-coded cell coordinates. Under load the Rust engine's move choice varies (minimax depth depends on available CPU), so the pre-recorded coordinate would already be occupied by the time Gatling sent it, producing a 409. The first 409 then cascaded: moves 3–12 would all fail instantly against a desynchronised board, meaning the test was really measuring the *API's validation rejection*, not the engine. The scenario now keeps only Move 1 (guaranteed valid on an empty board), so every virtual user puts real computation load onto the engine.
